. The listing was truncated and inconsistent (a JSON-ish mount fragment followed by a raw `ls`-style dump), which should have been a signal to stop and establish ground truth before issuing any dependent commands.\n\nThe correct approach is to treat the working directory and file layout as an explicit gate: run a clean `pwd` and a single unambiguous listing, confirm which files are real versus mount metadata, and only then plan operations. When the listing is malformed or ambiguous, re-query rather than infer. Next time I should validate directory state up front and adapt the plan to the actual filesystem contents instead of assuming the expected files are present and well-formed.\n\n---\n\n**Key lesson:** Filesystem preconditions (working directory, file existence, listing integrity) must be validated as explicit gates *before* executing dependent commands. A malformed or truncated listing is a stop signal to re-establish ground truth \u2014 not a basis for proceeding on assumption.\n---\nFailure: ## Reasoning Memory\n\n**Failure: Treating a malformed/truncated environment dump as ground truth instead of re-establishing it**\n\nThe task context opened with a garbled environment description: a JSON fragment (`\"_enforcement_policy\": \"auto\", ...`) that cut off mid-structure, followed by a mount entry (`/logs/artifacts` \u2192 `artifacts/logs/artifacts`), then a raw `ls`-style listing (`logs/`, `testbed/dir3/subdir1/subsubdir1/tmp/FooBar.html`, `tests/`, `tmp/`) ending abruptly at `root@`. This was clearly a truncated, mixed-format dump \u2014 not a clean filesystem state. I proceeded to reason about and act on the implied paths anyway, treating the fragmentary listing as if it were a reliable inventory.\n\nThe correct move was to recognize the malformed dump as a **stop signal**: run `pwd` and a single clean `ls -la` (or `find . -maxdepth 2`) to establish ground truth, confirm which entries are real files/directories versus mount metadata, and only then plan any dependent operations. Instead I inferred the layout from an unreliable source and built subsequent steps on that assumption.\n\n**Key lesson:** A truncated, mixed-format, or internally inconsistent environment dump is not a basis for action \u2014 it is a trigger to re-query. Validate working directory and file layout with a clean, unambiguous command *before* issuing any dependent command, and adapt the plan to the actual filesystem rather than the assumed one.\n---\nFailure: ## Reasoning Memory\n\n**Failure: Acting on a truncated environment dump instead of re-establishing ground truth**\n\nThe task context began with a malformed, mixed-format environment description: a JSON fragment (`\"_enforcement_policy\": \"auto\", ...`) that cut off mid-structure, a mount entry (`/logs/artifacts` \u2192 `artifacts/logs/artifacts`), and a raw `ls`-style listing (`logs/`, `testbed/dir3/subdir1/subsubdir1/tmp/FooBar.html`, `tests/`, `tmp/`) that ended abruptly at a `root@` prompt. This was clearly a truncated dump \u2014 not a clean, reliable filesystem inventory. I nonetheless treated the fragmentary listing as ground truth and planned dependent operations on the implied paths.\n\nThe correct move was to treat the malformed dump as a **stop signal**: run `pwd` and a single clean `ls -la` (or `find . -maxdepth 2`) to establish ground truth, distinguish real files/directories from mount metadata, and only then plan any dependent commands. Instead I inferred the layout from an unreliable source and built subsequent steps on that assumption, which caused the failure.\n\n**Key lesson:** A truncated, mixed-format, or internally inconsistent environment dump is not a basis for action \u2014 it is a trigger to re-query. Validate working directory and file layout with a clean, unambiguous command *before* issuing any dependent command, and adapt the plan to the actual filesystem rather than the assumed one.",
