            "carry": "Failure: **Failure: Acting on a truncated environment dump instead of re-establishing ground truth**\n\nThe task context opened with a malformed, mixed-format environment description: a JSON fragment (`\"_enforcement_policy\": \"auto\", ...`) that cut off mid-structure, a mount entry (`/logs/artifacts` \u2192 `artifacts/logs/artifacts`), and a raw `ls`-style listing (`logs/`, `testbed/dir3/subdir1/subsubdir1/tmp/FooBar.html`, `tests/`, `tmp/`) that ended abruptly at a `root@` prompt. This was clearly a truncated dump \u2014 not a clean, reliable filesystem inventory. I nonetheless treated the fragmentary listing as ground truth and planned dependent operations on the implied paths.\n\nThe correct move was to treat the malformed dump as a **stop signal**: run `pwd` and a single clean `ls -la` (or `find . -maxdepth 2`) to establish ground truth, distinguish real files/directories from mount metadata, and only then plan any dependent commands. Instead I inferred the layout from an unreliable source and built subsequent steps on that assumption, which caused the failure.\n\n**Key lesson:** A truncated, mixed-format, or internally inconsistent environment dump is not a basis for action \u2014 it is a trigger to re-query. Validate working directory and file layout with a clean, unambiguous command *before* issuing any dependent command, and adapt the plan to the actual filesystem rather than the assumed one.\n---\nFailure: **Failure: Acting on a truncated environment dump instead of re-establishing ground truth**\n\nThe task context opened with a malformed, mixed-format environment description: a JSON fragment (`\"_enforcement_policy\": \"auto\", ...`) that cut off mid-structure, a mount entry (`/logs/artifacts` \u2192 `artifacts/logs/artifacts`), and a raw `ls`-style listing (`logs/`, `testbed/dir3/subdir1/subsubdir1/tmp/FooBar.html`, `tests/`, `tmp/`) that ended abruptly at a `root@` prompt. This was clearly a truncated dump \u2014 not a clean, rel