            "carry": "Failure: **Reasoning memory: Failure \u2014 treating a truncated, mixed-format environment dump as ground truth**\n\nThe environment context arrived as a corrupted, mixed-format dump: a JSON fragment (`\"extra_docker_compose\": [], \"kwargs\": {}, ... \"verifier\": {...}`) that cut off mid-structure, a mount entry (`/logs/artifacts` \u2192 `artifacts/logs/artifacts`), and a raw `ls`-style listing (`logs/`, `tests/`, `tmp/`, `hello.c`, `hello.php`, ...) that terminated abruptly mid-line at `-rw-r--r`. This was unmistakably a truncated dump \u2014 not a clean filesystem inventory. I nonetheless treated the fragmentary listing as authoritative and planned dependent operations on the implied paths.\n\nThe correct move was to treat the malformed dump as a **stop signal**: run `pwd` and a single clean `ls -la` (or `find . -maxdepth 2`) to establish ground truth, separate real files/directories from mount metadata and JSON config fragments, and only then issue any dependent command. Instead I inferred the layout from an unreliable source and built subsequent steps on that assumption, which caused the failure.\n\n**Key lesson:** A truncated, mixed-format, or internally inconsistent environment dump is not a basis for action \u2014 it is a trigger to re-query. Validate working directory and file layout with a clean, unambiguous command *before* issuing any dependent command, and adapt the plan to the actual filesystem rather than the assumed one.\n---\nFailure: **Reasoning memory: Failure \u2014 treating a truncated, mixed-format environment dump as ground truth**\n\nThe environment context arrived as a corrupted, mixed-format dump: a JSON fragment (`\"extra_docker_compose\": [], \"kwargs\": {}, ... \"verifier\": {...}`) that cut off mid-structure, a mount entry (`/logs/artifacts` \u2192 `artifacts/logs/artifacts`), and a raw `ls`-style listing (`logs/`, `tests/`, `tmp/`, `hello.c`, `hello.php`, ...) that terminated abruptly mid-line at `-rw-r--r`. This