            "carry": "Failure: **What worked:** Reading the trace as a flat file listing rather than a sequence of events. The lines are `path timestamp size` records \u2014 `/system/html1.html`, `/system/text1.txt`, `/system/folder1/keep.txt`, `/system/folder3/backup_dbg/text1_dbg.txt`, `/system/folder1/doc1.doc`, `/system/folder1/log1.log`, `/system/MANIFEST`, `/system/folder1/data.csv` \u2014 each with a `2026-10-23 08:16` timestamp and a byte count. The leading `/folder3/temp/empty.txt` line is malformed relative to the rest (no timestamp/size split matching the others), which is itself a signal the capture is partial or the listing is concatenated from more than one source.\n\n**What failed:** The pull toward a story. A directory tree with `backup_dbg/`, `keep.txt`, `MANIFEST`, and a `log1.log` invites a fabricated episode (\"a backup ran, a debug copy of text1 was made, a manifest was written, logs were rotated\"). The listing supports none of that. It establishes only that at 08:16 on 2026-10-23 these paths existed with these sizes. It does not establish creation order, which process wrote them, whether `backup_dbg/text1_dbg.txt` is a copy of `text1.txt` (sizes differ: 17 vs 13), what `MANIFEST` contains, or whether anything was deleted. Treating filenames like `backup_dbg` or `keep.txt` as evidence of actions is the same scaffolding-as-events error as reading timeout multipliers as runtime.\n\n**Transferable lesson:** A file listing is state, not narrative. When the artifact is a directory snapshot (paths, timestamps, sizes) or any capture with a malformed/truncated line, the honest output is a description of what it does and does not establish \u2014 here, a set of files under `/system` with a common 08:16 timestamp, one apparent debug copy, and a manifest \u2014 plus a redirect (request the actual log contents, the manifest body, or a diff between `text1.txt` and `text1_dbg.txt`) rather than reconstructing an episode the evidence can't support.\n---\nFailure: **What worked:** Reading the trace as a raw command-and-output capture rather than a coherent session. The line begins with a shell prompt (`75008:/#`) followed by a `find /system -type f -print` invocation, and then a run of paths. Critically, the paths after the command are *not* newline-separated in the way a real `find` output would be \u2014 they run together (`/system/folder3/special text4.txt`, `/system/folder2/special text3.txt`), and the command itself is malformed: `find /system -type f -print /system/folder3/special text4.txt ...` puts the extra paths as arguments to `find`, not as output. So the \"listing\" is partly the command line and partly a path dump, and the boundary between them is ambiguous. That ambiguity is the signal.\n\n**What failed:** The pull toward treating the path run as a clean directory snapshot. It is tempting to read off a tree \u2014 `folder3/backup_dbg/` with `sql1.sql` and `text1_dbg.txt`, `folder3/temp/empty.txt`, `folder2/special_text1.txt` and `special_text2.txt`, a `.DS_Store`, an `a.out`, a `MANIFEST` \u2014 and narrate (\"a backup ran, debug copies were made, temp files were staged, a build artifact exists\"). The trace supports almost none of that. It does not establish that `find` actually ran successfully, that these are all files (the `-type f` filter is in the command, but the output is not cleanly separated from the arguments), what the sizes or timestamps are (none shown here, unlike the prior listing), or whether `special text4.txt` and `special text3.txt` \u2014 with embedded spaces \u2014 are single paths or two tokens each. The `a.out` and `.DS_Store` invite a \"compiled and used a Mac\" story; the `backup_dbg` directory invites a \"backup episode.\" Both are scaffolding.\n\n**Transferable lesson:** When a trace interleaves a command with its output and the boundary is not clean (paths concatenated, arguments mixed with results, filenames containing spaces), do not silently parse it into a tidy listing. State the ambiguity explicitly: here, a `find /system -type f -print` was issued, followed by a run of paths that may be output, may be arguments, or a mix; the capture does not cleanly delimit them, and no sizes/timestamps are present to cross-check. The honest move is to describe what is and isn't established and redirect \u2014 request a clean `find` output with `-printf '%p %s %T@\\n'`, or `ls -laR /system`, or the actual transcript with prompt/output separation \u2014 rather than reconstructing a filesystem episode the evidence can't support.",
