 ... \"extra_allowed_hosts\": [] }, \"verifier\": { \"disable\": false, \"environment_mode\": \"shared\" } } [ { \"source\": \"/logs/artifacts\", ... } ] ?? logs/` \u2014 with no opening brace, no enclosing key, and no closing brace. The temptation is to read it as a finished config: \"a task with delete/stream flags, auto enforcement policies, a shared verifier, and an artifacts mount.\" But the fragment begins mid-object (the leading `\"delete\": true` has no opener), the `}` after `extra_allowed_hosts` closes an object whose opener is absent, the `}` after `environment_mode` closes the verifier but leaves the parent unclosed, and the trailing `?? logs/` is a truncated token with no structural meaning. There is no way to tell whether the verifier block belongs to the same object as the delete/stream flags, or to a parent, or to a sibling in an array \u2014 and the `[ { \"source\": ... } ]` array floats with no key binding it.\n\n**Transferable lesson:** When a trace starts or ends mid-token \u2014 a bare key-value with no opening brace, a `}` with no matching `{`, a trailing `??` fragment \u2014 do not promote the visible fields into a coherent record. Name them as fragments (a set of boolean/policy flags, a verifier sub-object, an artifacts mount array) and state explicitly that the object boundaries are unmarked, so field ownership and completeness are unknown. Then redirect: request the full JSON document, or the enclosing task manifest, or a pretty-printed config with balanced braces. Assembling a \"task with these flags and this verifier\" story from a headless object tail is exactly the reconstruction the evidence cannot support.\n---\nFailure: **What failed:** Treating a headless JSON tail plus a bare file listing as a single coherent task record. The trace opens with `}, \"verifier\": { \"disable\": false, \"environment_mode\": \"shared\" } }` \u2014 a closing brace with no opener, a verifier sub-object, then a second unmatched `}` \u2014 followed by a floating array `[ { \"source\": \"/logs/artifacts\", ... } ]` with no key binding it, then a run of `??`-prefixed tokens (`?? logs/`, `?? mytarfile.tgz`, `?? tests/`, `?? tmp/`) and a stream of `f /system/folder1/...` lines. The temptation is to narrate this as \"a task with a shared verifier, an artifacts mount, and a set of untracked files plus a file manifest.\" But every structural boundary is missing: the leading `}` closes an object whose opener is absent, the verifier's parent is unclosed, the artifacts array floats unkeyed, the `??` tokens are truncated status markers with no enclosing command, and the `f /path` lines are a bare listing with no header declaring what `f` means or which root they belong to. There is no way to tell whether the verifier, the artifacts mount, the untracked files, and the file listing belong to the same task, the same directory, or four unrelated fragments concatenated by a truncated stream.\n\n**Transferable lesson:** When a trace is a concatenation of orphaned fragments \u2014 a `}` with no `{`, an unkeyed array, `??` status tokens with no command, `f /path` lines with no manifest header \u2014 do not stitch them into one narrative. Name each fragment for what it is (a verifier sub-object tail, an unbound artifacts array, a set of untracked-path markers, a bare file listing) and state explicitly that the boundaries between them are unmarked, so ownership, ordering, and completeness are unknown. Then redirect: request the full JSON document with balanced braces, the enclosing task manifest, the complete `git status`/`ls` output with its command line, or a pretty-printed config. Assembling \"a task with this verifier and these files\" from a pile of headless tails is exactly the reconstruction the evidence cannot support.",
